; SWD (Serial Wire Debug) controller for the MVP chip -- task B1-FW-swd.
;
; The chip plays the CONTROLLER, i.e. the debug probe: it generates SWCLK and
; drives SWDIO, and the device on the other end is an SW-DP.  The judge is
; verif/vip/swd.py: `SwdTarget` is the DP under test and `SwdChecker` watches the
; two nets.  Nothing here is taken from an RP2040 example; every field below
; comes from docs/mvp-isa.md and research/ref/protocols/swd/.
;
; Where the pins are (fw/swd/driver.py builds PINCTRL out of these)
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;   SIDESET_BASE = SWCLK pin, SIDESET_COUNT = 1, no `opt`, SIDE_EN = 0
;       Every instruction side-sets bit 0 to SWCLK, so `side 1` is the high half
;       of a bit period and an instruction with no `side` operand is the low
;       half.  One side-set bit is all a clock needs, and it costs no window.
;   OUT_BASE = SWDIO pin, OUT_COUNT = 1
;       `out pindirs, 1` writes SWDIO's *direction* from one OSR bit: 1 = pull
;       low, 0 = release (docs/mvp-isa.md `### Open drain`).  ADIv5 lets both
;       sides pull SWDIO low, so open drain is the only correct output mode and
;       SWDIO's level register stays 0.
;   IN_BASE = SWDIO pin: `in pins, 1` samples the line.
;   SET_BASE = FLAG pin, SET_COUNT = 1
;       `jmp pin` reads FLAG (EXECCTRL.JMP_PIN) and that is how the host says
;       "this transfer is a write": it forces `set pins, 1` or `set pins, 0`
;       through SMx_INSTR before it jumps to `xfer`.
;   CLKDIV = the bit period in core cycles; see HIGH/LOW below.
;
; SHIFTCTRL: PULL_THRESH 31, which is what `jmp !osre` at `e1` compares against --
; osr_cnt is 9 after the request run and 31 after WDATA, and 31 is the only
; threshold that separates them.  AUTOPULL and AUTOPUSH are off (the program pulls
; and pushes explicitly), OUT_SHIFTDIR 1 so `out pindirs, 1` sends OSR bit 0
; first, which is the wire order, and IN_SHIFTDIR 0 so that 32 `in pins, 1` leave
; the received word in bits 31:0 with bit k = data bit k.  PUSH_THRESH is 1, so
; `push iffull` hands the ISR over when a receive has filled it and does nothing
; when the phase that just ended transmitted instead.
;
; The bit period
; -------------
; One bit period is `3 + HIGH + LOW` core cycles: a loop body is three
; instructions -- the level change, the clock high and the `jmp` back -- and
; CLKDIV is 1.0, so one SM cycle is one core cycle and the two delays are the rest
; of the period.  `tx` and `p2` put the level on the line *before* the clock goes
; high and `ck`/`rx` put the clock high first, which is what each of them needs:
; a transmitted bit has to be there before its rising edge, and a sampled one has
; to be read after it.  The host picks HIGH and LOW
; (fw/swd/driver.py `retarget`).  A loop body is 2 words, so a 32-bit data phase
; costs 3 words instead of 32; that is the only reason a bit-bang controller fits
; in 32 words at all (fw/swd/LIMITS.md has the word budget).
;
; HIGH also has to cover the input path.  The DP answers one tick after it sees
; the rising edge, and this chip reads SWDIO T_IN = 3 ticks after the pad
; (docs/mvp-isa.md `## Timing`), so `in pins, 1` runs HIGH + 1 cycles after the
; edge whose bit it samples.  HIGH >= 5 is enough; the driver never goes below 5.
;
; The phases
; ----------
; A transfer is a sequence of runs of whole bit periods, and each run is one loop
; or one unrolled pair:
;
;   xfer      9 periods  the request byte (8 bits) plus the turnaround
;   wack/ck   3 periods  the ACK, clocked and not sampled
;   rd/rx    32 periods  the RDATA bits, sampled into the ISR
;   p2        3 periods  RDATA parity, RDATA turnaround, turnaround pull-down
;
; and a write sends 31 of the 33 WDATA bits from the `tx` loop, bit 31 and the
; parity bit coming out of `p2`.  Every run length here is one below the number of
; bit periods, because `set x, n` is n + 1 loop bodies -- which is also why the
; longest run this program needs, 32 receive periods, is `set x, 30` and not out
; of reach.  A read therefore clocks 46 periods, which is ADIv5's read frame exactly
; (REQ 8, Trn 1, ACK 3, DATA 33, Trn 1); a write clocks 46 as well, against 47
; for a write frame, and the one it does not clock is the trailing turnaround the
; DP does not need: it has already seen a low line by then.
;
; Three loops (tx, ck, rx) rather than one loop with a mode bit, because every
; loop then has its *own* exit address and the phase after a run is simply the
; instruction that follows that loop.  One shared loop would need a phase flag in
; a register, and X, Y, ISR and OSR are all busy.
;
; Two exits still have to know something:
;   * `e1` is the exit of `tx`, which a write runs twice (the request, then
;     WDATA).  `jmp !osre` separates them, as above.
;   * `e2` is the exit of `ck`, and it needs the host's read/write flag.  That flag
;     lives on a pin, read with `jmp pin`, because there is no free register and no
;     IRQ to park it in.
; The exit of `rx` needs nothing: WRAP_TOP is its `jmp` and WRAP_BOTTOM is the
; `push iffull` behind `e1`, so falling off the end of the loop lands there.  The
; driver works both of those out from the labels (fw/swd/driver.py
; `execctrl_word`), and `wpre` is placed after the loop because of it.
;
; A line reset needs no entry of its own: `xfer` with a word of zeros clocks 46
; periods with SWDIO released (the request bits are all "released", the ACK loop
; does not touch the line, the receive loop only samples, and `p2` releases as
; well), which is most of the 50 clocks ADIv5 asks for [S12].  The host runs it
; twice and asks `p2` for a low clock at the end of the second, which is what
; brings the DP out of its reset state ([S11], and the IDCODE read that has to
; follow it is [S13]).

.program swd
.side_set 1

.define HIGH 6              ; clock-high delay: SWCLK high for HIGH + 1 cycles
.define LOW 1               ; clock-low delay: the rest of the period

PUBLIC xfer:
        pull block           ; the request word from the TX FIFO.  It is the 8-bit
                              ; header already inverted for the open-drain output
                              ; (1 = release) in bits 7:0, and bit 8 = 0 so the
                              ; ninth period, which is the turnaround, leaves SWDIO
                              ; to the pull-up.
        set x, 8             ; nine bit periods.  `set x, n` is n + 1 loop bodies: the
                              ; body runs before the `jmp x--` that tests it, so the
                              ; counter has to be one below the length
        jmp tx

; ---- transmit loop: one period, one OSR bit on SWDIO ----------------------
tx:     out pindirs, 1 [LOW] ; SWDIO's direction from OSR bit 0, put on the line while
                              ; SWCLK is still low -- ADIv5 changes SWDIO at the falling
                              ; edge of SWCLK and never while it is high [S19], and the
                              ; bit has to be there before this period's rising edge
        nop side 1 [HIGH]    ; SWCLK high: the bit is on the wire
        jmp x-- tx           ; one more low cycle, then the next period

; ---- exit of `tx`: the request run and the WDATA run both land here ---------
e1:     jmp !osre wack       ; osr_cnt is 9 after the request and 32 after WDATA,
                              ; and PULL_THRESH is 31, so this is "the request is on
                              ; the wire"
        push iffull          ; WDATA is finished and the ISR is empty, so this does
                              ; nothing.  For a read the same instruction is reached
                              ; through the wrap, with 32 RDATA bits in the ISR, and
                              ; it is what hands them to the host.

; ---- three closing periods, each carrying one OSR bit ----------------------
p2:     pull block           ; the closing word, bits 2:0 (see the driver's
                              ; `closing_word`): a read releases all three, a write
                              ; sends data bit 31, then the WDATA parity bit, then
                              ; pulls the line low
        out pindirs, 1 [LOW] ; period 45
        nop side 1 [HIGH]
        out pindirs, 1 [LOW] ; period 46
        nop side 1 [HIGH]
        out pindirs, 1 [LOW] ; period 47: pull SWDIO low, which is the clock with a
                              ; low line that the DP needs before it will look at a
                              ; new header.  Same order as `tx`: the level goes on the
                              ; line first, while the clock is still low.
        nop side 1 [HIGH]    ; the rising edge of period 47
done:   jmp done [15]        ; park with SWCLK low.

; ---- the ACK, clocked and deliberately not sampled -------------------------
wack:   set x, 1 [(LOW - 1)]    ; TWO periods, not three, and that is the whole
                              ; subtlety of driving this link.  The DP reacts to a
                              ; rising edge one tick after the edge and its answer is
                              ; on the pad two ticks after it (verif/vip/core.py, "so a
                              ; device reacts one tick after it sees a change"), so the
                              ; bit it computes at a rising edge is already on the line
                              ; inside that same edge's high window.  The third ACK bit
                              ; and the first RDATA bit therefore land in the window
                              ; after the second, and a controller that clocks three
                              ; periods for the ACK misses the first data bit.  The
                              ; same is why a write needs no turnaround period here:
                              ; the DP reads WDATA bit 0 in the window this loop's
                              ; second period would have occupied.
        jmp ck               ; Nothing samples the ACK here; `verif.vip.swd.
                              ; SwdChecker` is what judges the response.
                              ; The `[LOW - 1]` on the line above is what keeps every
                              ; bit period the same length: the gap between the last
                              ; rising edge of `tx` and the first one of `ck` is four
                              ; instructions wide, so it needs LOW - 1 cycles of delay to
                              ; come out at HIGH + LOW + 3 like every other period.  It is
                              ; the only place the program has to think about where a
                              ; phase boundary is.
ck:     nop side 1 [HIGH]
        nop [LOW]            ; SWCLK low, and SWDIO untouched, so the DP owns it
        jmp x-- ck

; ---- exit of `ck`: the host's flag says read or write ----------------------
e2:     jmp pin wpre         ; FLAG = 1 means a write; the host forced
                              ; `set pins, 1` before `jmp xfer`.  `jmp pin` reads
                              ; EXECCTRL.JMP_PIN, it takes no operand.
        set x, 31            ; 32 receive periods

; ---- receive loop: one period, one SWDIO bit into the ISR -----------------
rx:     nop side 1 [HIGH]    ; SWCLK high.  The read path above falls straight in.
        in pins, 1 [LOW]     ; SWCLK low, and the bit is in ISR bit 0.  With
                              ; IN_SHIFTDIR 0 the first bit sampled ends up in bit
                              ; 31, so 32 samples leave the word in bits 31:0 in
                              ; wire order.
        jmp x-- rx           ; the exit is the wrap, onto the `push iffull` above.

; ---- write: the turnaround, then 31 of the 33 WDATA bits ------------------
wpre:   nop side 1 [HIGH]    ; the ACK/WDATA turnaround, which a write needs and a read
        nop [LOW]            ; does not: the DP reads WDATA bit 0 from the window after
                              ; the one it turns around in, so its first `tx` period has
                              ; to be a period with SWDIO released
        pull block           ; the WDATA word, inverted for the open-drain output
        set x, 31            ; 32 periods: WDATA bits 0 to 31.  The parity bit and
                              ; the turnaround after it come out of `p2`.
        jmp tx               ; back to `tx`; `e1` sees osr_cnt = 32 and falls
                              ; through to `p2`
